**user**:
> Ekemon wants to make a keep with followers. What kinds of rules are there for that?
**assistant**:
> In **official 5e**, there are a few overlapping ways to handle this—but no single complete “kingdom-management” system. For Ekemon, I’d use a light campaign system now, then graduate to the newer **Bastion** rules once he is stronger.
> 
> ## Official 5e options
> 
> ### 1. Build a literal keep — *2014 Dungeon Master’s Guide*
> The original DMG has construction prices:
> 
> | Building | Cost | Construction time |
> |---|---:|---:|
> | Fortified tower | 15,000 gp | 100 days |
> | **Keep** | **150,000 gp** | **400 days** |
> | Castle | 500,000 gp | 1,200 days |
> 
> That is the huge endgame version: Ekemon will need land, builders, guards, and lots of treasure. Very Titan-like, but not a near-term purchase.
> 
> ### 2. Bastions — *2024 Dungeon Master’s Guide*
> The 2024 rules include **Bastions**, a much better official system for a hero’s home base.
> 
> - Normally begins at **level 5**.
> - A character gets a base with rooms/facilities: barracks, armory, workshop, library, garden, sanctuary, etc.
> - Between adventures, Ekemon gives each facility an order: make something, train people, gather rumors, research a relic, improve defenses.
> - Over time the bastion gains upgrades and can attract staff.
> 
> It is designed for “my character has a cool headquarters,” rather than tedious accounting. We can absolutely say that Ekemon is *planning* his bastion now, and unlock it mechanically at level 5.
> 
> ### 3. Hirelings, guards, and sidekicks
> Even at low levels, Ekemon can hire people:
> 
> - **Laborers / builders** for construction
> - **Guards** for watch duty
> - **A steward** to manage supplies and messages
> - **Specialists** such as a blacksmith, sage, healer, or scout
> - **Sidekicks** for named adventure companions—Varkash is ideal for this
> 
> The important distinction: ordinary followers should be **voluntary employees, friends, rescued people, or fellow Titan-seekers**, not magically controlled servants. Varkash’s sports-contract can be a voluntary champion agreement with clear exit rules.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Recommended: Ekemon’s Titan Keep Rules
> 
> For a six-year-old-friendly campaign, I’d make the keep a **visible reward track** instead of an economics simulation.
> 
> ## Phase 1: The Titan Camp — now
> Ekemon finds, wins, buys, or is granted an old **watchtower / cliff cave / ruined shrine**. It is not a real keep yet, but it is his place.
> 
> He can have up to **three followers** at first:
> 
> 1. **Varkash Redscale** — Champion and sparring partner  
> 2. **Grima or another craftsperson** — Builder / smith / relic-helper  
> 3. **A scout or animal friend** — Finds rumors and watches the road  
> 
> Each follower gets one simple job between adventures:
> 
> | Follower job | What it can do |
> |---|---|
> | **Guard** | Warns Ekemon of danger; helps defend the base |
> | **Scout** | Finds one rumor, trail, or possible treasure |
> | **Builder** | Repairs or adds one small upgrade |
> | **Crafter** | Makes a simple item or improves equipment |
> | **Lorekeeper** | Identifies Titan clues and old magic |
> | **Healer / Gardener** | Provides potions, food, or a safe resting place |
> 
> **Simple rule:** after each adventure, Ekemon chooses **one follower job**. Roll only if the outcome would be exciting or uncertain.
> 
> ## Phase 2: The Redclaw Hold — around levels 3–4
> After earning treasure and local respect, the camp becomes a small defended stronghold.
> 
> Choose **one upgrade** after every major Titan relic or major victory:
> 
> - **Training Yard:** Ekemon and Varkash can train; once per adventure, one gets a small bonus on a physical challenge.
> - **Smithy:** repair gear, make a club, or craft a basic special weapon.
> - **Dragon Watchtower:** see approaching dragons, raiders, or storms early.
> - **Titan Shrine:** Titan Seed gives strange visions or clues toward the next relic.
> - **Guest Hall:** more allies can stay safely; local people begin asking Ekemon for help.
> - **Hidden Tunnel:** a dramatic escape route—or a secret route into danger.
> 
> This can happen largely through story rewards, captured materials, and favors—not only gold.
> 
> ## Phase 3: Titan Keep — level 5+
> At level 5, switch to the official **Bastion** system and call Ekemon’s Bastion **Titan Keep**.
> 
> By then, it might have:
> 
> - **The Hall of the Titan Seed**
> - **Varkash’s Champion Yard**
> - **The Fourfold Courtyard**: air, earth, fire, and water shrines
> - **The Redclaw Forge**
> - **A storm-facing tower** for the Canyon of Storms storyline
> 
> ---
> 
> # The Followers’ Code
> 
> Since Ekemon is playfully “chaotic evil,” I’d give him a villainous-but-heroic code that makes having followers fun rather than mean:
> 
> 1. **No follower is trapped forever.** They can leave after finishing their agreement.
> 2. **Ekemon pays, protects, or rewards his crew.**
> 3. **Everyone gets a job, a bed, and a share of treasure.**
> 4. **If someone challenges Ekemon fairly, he hears them out—or settles it in a contest.**
> 5. **Titan Keep protects its people from bullies, monsters, and boring kings.**
> 
> That makes him a spectacularly dramatic future Titan-lord—not simply a boss who orders everyone around.
> 
> ## A very simple “Loyalty” rule
> For named followers such as Varkash, give each **3 Trust stars**.
> 
> - Ekemon keeps promises, shares treasure, rescues them, or listens to them: **gain 1 star**.
> - Ekemon breaks a promise, embarrasses them, or sends them into needless danger: **lose 1 star**.
> - At **3 stars**, that follower will take a brave risk for Ekemon.
> - At **0 stars**, they quit, demand a contest, or become a temporary rival—not an enemy by default.
> 
> This turns followers into characters with opinions, which is much more fun.
> 
